handler yields a \emph{recent-cases-only} corpus (~235 environmental-assessment
issues total at the time of writing). Plan accordingly.
}

\section{Three processes merged (assessment_type)}{

The portal models every dossier as a unified \code{Issue}; environmental
assessment lives in three of its \code{process}es, selected server-side by
\code{processId}:
\itemize{
\item \code{15} — \emph{Tilkynning til ákvörðunar um matsskyldu} (EIA-track screening),
tagged \code{assessment_type = "EIA"}.
\item \code{16} — \emph{Mat á umhverfisáhrifum} (full EIA), tagged
\code{assessment_type = "EIA"}.
\item \code{501} — \emph{Umhverfismat áætlana} (SEA), tagged \code{assessment_type = "SEA"}.
}

The three are merged into one result tibble. \code{assessment_type}
(\code{"EIA"}/\code{"SEA"}) tags each row and is preserved in the offline metadata cache; the finer
distinction is kept in \code{process_type} / \code{native_type}. Because the three
processes share the same \code{Issue.id} space, \code{document_id} is prefixed with
the process id (\verb{IS-15-<id>}, \verb{IS-16-<id>}, \verb{IS-501-<id>}) so they never
collide on disk. The \code{assessment_type} argument (\code{"All"}/\code{"EIA"}/\code{"SEA"})
selects which processes to crawl: \code{"EIA"} -> \verb{\{15, 16\}}, \code{"SEA"} -> \code{{501}}.
